1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,675
This is MATH 239, video 12. The topic is binary strings, regular expressions, and ambiguity. It covers sections 3.1 to 3.2.1 of the course notes.

2
00:00:14,125 --> 00:00:19,842
After this video, you can read a regular expression and list the strings that it produces.

3
00:00:20,292 --> 00:00:24,239
You can decide if an expression is ambiguous, and prove it.

4
00:00:24,689 --> 00:00:29,446
You can turn an unambiguous expression into a generating series.

5
00:00:29,896 --> 00:00:37,128
And you can solve practice problem P4-1 and Practice Midterm question 6, parts a and b.

6
00:00:37,578 --> 00:00:45,834
A binary string is a finite sequence of bits. Each bit is 0 or 1. The number of bits is the length of the string.

7
00:00:46,284 --> 00:00:51,575
There is exactly one string of length 0. It is the empty string, epsilon.

8
00:00:52,025 --> 00:00:55,139
Length 1 gives two strings, 0 and 1.

9
00:00:55,589 --> 00:01:01,691
The set of all binary strings is the union of the sets of strings of length n, over all n.

10
00:01:02,141 --> 00:01:04,210
Length 2 gives four strings.

11
00:01:04,660 --> 00:01:06,623
Length 3 gives eight strings.

12
00:01:07,073 --> 00:01:13,729
In general, there are 2 to the n strings of length n, because each of the n bits has two choices.

13
00:01:14,179 --> 00:01:25,016
So the generating series of all binary strings, with respect to length, is the sum of 2 to the n x to the n. That is one over one minus 2x.

14
00:01:25,466 --> 00:01:29,199
In this chapter, the weight of a string is always its length.

15
00:01:29,649 --> 00:01:36,860
A regular expression is defined recursively. Epsilon, 0 and 1 are regular expressions.

16
00:01:37,310 --> 00:01:45,246
If R and S are regular expressions, then R or S is one, and R S, the concatenation, is one.

17
00:01:45,696 --> 00:01:48,491
R to the k means k copies of R.

18
00:01:48,941 --> 00:01:53,101
And if R is a regular expression, then R star is one.

19
00:01:53,551 --> 00:02:01,551
Here is the expression 1 star, 0 1 star, star. Its top operation is a concatenation of two parts.

20
00:02:02,001 --> 00:02:03,985
The first part is 1 star.

21
00:02:04,435 --> 00:02:07,251
The 1 below it is a basic expression.

22
00:02:07,701 --> 00:02:10,901
The second part is the star of 0 1 star.

23
00:02:11,351 --> 00:02:15,020
Inside it, 0 is concatenated with 1 star.

24
00:02:15,470 --> 00:02:19,822
Each node of the tree uses one rule of Definition 3.2.

25
00:02:20,272 --> 00:02:22,661
The symbol that looks like a cup means or.

26
00:02:23,111 --> 00:02:31,367
An expression is only a formal construction. We read it in two ways: as a set of strings, and as a rational function.

27
00:02:31,817 --> 00:02:37,620
The concatenation of alpha and beta is the bits of alpha followed by the bits of beta.

28
00:02:38,070 --> 00:02:46,006
For two sets of strings, A B is the set of all concatenations alpha beta, with alpha in A and beta in B.

29
00:02:46,456 --> 00:02:52,003
Example 3.4. The set A has the strings 011 and 01.

30
00:02:52,453 --> 00:02:57,509
The set B has the strings 101 and 1101.

31
00:02:57,959 --> 00:03:01,095
First pair: 011 then 101.

32
00:03:01,545 --> 00:03:03,465
The orange cells come from A.

33
00:03:03,915 --> 00:03:07,136
The purple line marks the cut between the two parts.

34
00:03:07,586 --> 00:03:11,874
Second pair: 011 then 1101.

35
00:03:12,324 --> 00:03:15,439
Third pair: 01 then 101.

36
00:03:15,889 --> 00:03:19,793
Fourth pair: 01 then 1101.

37
00:03:20,243 --> 00:03:32,872
Look at the two red boxes. 011 dot 101 and 01 dot 1101 give the same string, 011101.

38
00:03:33,322 --> 00:03:36,095
The result does not record where the cut was.

39
00:03:36,545 --> 00:03:39,020
So the set A B has only 3 strings.

40
00:03:39,470 --> 00:03:41,497
But A cross B has 4 pairs.

41
00:03:41,947 --> 00:03:48,880
So the concatenation product is not the Cartesian product. This fact is the source of ambiguity.

42
00:03:49,330 --> 00:03:53,874
Now the first reading. Each expression produces a set of strings.

43
00:03:54,324 --> 00:03:58,633
Epsilon, 0 and 1 produce the sets with that one string.

44
00:03:59,083 --> 00:04:04,097
As an example, let R produce 0 and 10, and let S produce 1.

45
00:04:04,547 --> 00:04:09,603
R or S produces the union of the two sets: 0, 10, and 1.

46
00:04:10,053 --> 00:04:15,706
R S produces the concatenation product: 01 and 101.

47
00:04:16,156 --> 00:04:20,401
R star produces the union of all the powers R to the k.

48
00:04:20,851 --> 00:04:23,390
R to the 0 is the empty string.

49
00:04:23,840 --> 00:04:25,952
R to the 1 is R itself.

50
00:04:26,402 --> 00:04:33,101
R to the 2 has four strings: 00, 010, 100, and 1010.

51
00:04:33,551 --> 00:04:36,324
The union of all these powers is R star.

52
00:04:36,774 --> 00:04:42,790
A set of strings that some regular expression produces is called a rational language.

53
00:04:43,240 --> 00:04:46,397
Many different expressions can produce the same set.

54
00:04:46,847 --> 00:04:51,541
1 star produces all strings of 1s, including the empty string.

55
00:04:51,991 --> 00:04:59,521
1 or 11, star, produces the same set. Each string of 1s can be built from pieces 1 and 11.

56
00:04:59,971 --> 00:05:10,531
1 then 11 star produces the strings of 1s of odd length: 1, 111, 11111, and so on.

57
00:05:10,981 --> 00:05:16,251
0 or 1, star, produces every binary string. Each piece is one bit.

58
00:05:16,701 --> 00:05:22,119
1 star, 0 1 star, star, also produces every binary string.

59
00:05:22,569 --> 00:05:26,900
So two different expressions can produce the same rational language.

60
00:05:27,350 --> 00:05:36,011
01 star produces the empty string, 01, 0101, and so on. It is a rational language.

61
00:05:36,461 --> 00:05:41,091
The second set has j zeros followed by j ones, for each j.

62
00:05:41,541 --> 00:05:44,954
Both sets have exactly one string of each even length.

63
00:05:45,404 --> 00:05:47,943
So they have the same generating series.

64
00:05:48,393 --> 00:05:51,422
But the second set is not a rational language.

65
00:05:51,872 --> 00:06:00,469
To produce it, we would need the union over all j of 0 to the j, 1 to the j. That is an infinite union.

66
00:06:00,919 --> 00:06:07,810
Definition 3.2 allows only finitely many operations, so it does not allow this.

67
00:06:08,260 --> 00:06:16,004
An expression is unambiguous if it produces every string of its set exactly once. Otherwise it is ambiguous.

68
00:06:16,454 --> 00:06:20,465
Ambiguity is a property of the expression, not of the set.

69
00:06:20,915 --> 00:06:25,821
1 star produces 111 in one way: three copies of 1.

70
00:06:26,271 --> 00:06:33,802
1 or 11, star, also produces 111. One way is three pieces: 1, 1, 1.

71
00:06:34,252 --> 00:06:36,471
A second way is 1 then 11.

72
00:06:36,921 --> 00:06:39,054
A third way is 11 then 1.

73
00:06:39,504 --> 00:06:43,451
So this expression produces 111 three times.

74
00:06:43,901 --> 00:06:48,274
It is ambiguous, but it produces the same set as 1 star.

75
00:06:48,724 --> 00:06:56,937
Lemma 3.9 decides ambiguity one operation at a time. Assume R and S are unambiguous.

76
00:06:57,387 --> 00:07:01,163
First, epsilon, 0 and 1 are unambiguous.

77
00:07:01,613 --> 00:07:06,605
R or S is unambiguous if and only if the two sets are disjoint.

78
00:07:07,055 --> 00:07:10,362
If a string is in both sets, it is produced twice.

79
00:07:10,812 --> 00:07:19,729
R S is unambiguous if and only if each string in the product has exactly one cut into a string of R and a string of S.

80
00:07:20,179 --> 00:07:28,435
In Example 3.4, the string 011101 has two cuts. So that product is ambiguous.

81
00:07:28,885 --> 00:07:33,536
With exactly one cut, R S matches R cross S, one to one.

82
00:07:33,986 --> 00:07:40,258
R star is unambiguous if and only if each power R to the k is unambiguous,

83
00:07:40,708 --> 00:07:43,289
and no string is in two different powers.

84
00:07:43,739 --> 00:07:48,753
Now prove that 1 star, 0 1 star, star, is unambiguous.

85
00:07:49,203 --> 00:07:54,600
Take the string 1101001. The zeros are orange.

86
00:07:55,050 --> 00:07:59,893
Each piece 0 1 star starts with a 0, and has no other 0.

87
00:08:00,343 --> 00:08:05,271
So every piece must start exactly at a 0. Cut before every 0.

88
00:08:05,721 --> 00:08:10,243
The pieces are forced. The first part, 11, comes from 1 star.

89
00:08:10,693 --> 00:08:16,432
The other parts, 01, 0, and 01, each come from 0 1 star.

90
00:08:16,882 --> 00:08:26,397
Inside the piece 01, the 0 comes first, and then 1 star gives one 1. So the piece also fixes the number of 1s.

91
00:08:26,847 --> 00:08:32,201
The string has 3 zeros, so it uses exactly 3 pieces 0 1 star.

92
00:08:32,651 --> 00:08:39,649
A string in the k-th power has exactly k zeros. So no string is in two different powers.

93
00:08:40,099 --> 00:08:47,523
The first factor, 1 star, takes the 1s before the first 0. So each string is produced exactly once.

94
00:08:47,973 --> 00:08:55,802
0 or 1, star, is easier. Each piece is one bit, so there is a cut after every bit. There is only one way.

95
00:08:56,252 --> 00:09:00,497
The second reading. Each expression leads to a rational function.

96
00:09:00,947 --> 00:09:02,761
Epsilon leads to 1.

97
00:09:03,211 --> 00:09:05,493
0 and 1 each lead to x.

98
00:09:05,943 --> 00:09:07,522
Or leads to a sum.

99
00:09:07,972 --> 00:09:10,447
Concatenation leads to a product.

100
00:09:10,897 --> 00:09:14,054
Star leads to one over one minus R of x.

101
00:09:14,504 --> 00:09:21,053
Work up the tree of 1 star, 0 1 star, star. Each bit is a leaf, and it leads to x.

102
00:09:21,503 --> 00:09:24,682
1 star leads to one over one minus x.

103
00:09:25,132 --> 00:09:32,535
0 then 1 star leads to x times one over one minus x, which is x over one minus x.

104
00:09:32,985 --> 00:09:37,934
The star of that leads to one over, one minus x over one minus x.

105
00:09:38,384 --> 00:09:46,043
Multiply top and bottom by one minus x. The result is one minus x, over one minus 2x.

106
00:09:46,493 --> 00:09:54,770
At the root, multiply the two parts: one over one minus x, times one minus x over one minus 2x.

107
00:09:55,220 --> 00:10:00,511
The one minus x cancels, and the result is one over one minus 2x.

108
00:10:00,961 --> 00:10:07,211
This is the sum of 2 to the n x to the n: the generating series of all binary strings.

109
00:10:07,661 --> 00:10:15,107
Example 3.12 compares four expressions. 1 star leads to one over one minus x.

110
00:10:15,557 --> 00:10:21,637
All its coefficients are 1. That is correct: there is one string of 1s of each length.

111
00:10:22,087 --> 00:10:33,564
1 or 11, star. The piece 1 leads to x, and the piece 11 leads to x squared. So the function is one over one minus x minus x squared.

112
00:10:34,014 --> 00:10:37,598
Its coefficients are 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8.

113
00:10:38,048 --> 00:10:43,595
But this expression produces only one string of each length. So these numbers are wrong.

114
00:10:44,045 --> 00:10:52,578
0 or 1, star. Each piece leads to x, so the sum is 2x. The function is one over one minus 2x.

115
00:10:53,028 --> 00:10:58,511
Its coefficients are the powers of 2: 1, 2, 4, 8. That is correct.

116
00:10:58,961 --> 00:11:06,534
1 star, 0 1 star, star, also leads to one over one minus 2x, as on the last slide.

117
00:11:06,984 --> 00:11:14,792
Both expressions produce all binary strings, with 2 to the n strings of length n. So both results are correct.

118
00:11:15,242 --> 00:11:26,975
Theorem 3.13. If R is an unambiguous expression, then its rational function equals the generating series of its set, with respect to length.

119
00:11:27,425 --> 00:11:31,244
The proof goes up the expression, one operation at a time.

120
00:11:31,694 --> 00:11:37,475
An unambiguous or is a disjoint union. So by the Sum Lemma, the series add.

121
00:11:37,925 --> 00:11:44,048
An unambiguous concatenation gives a bijection between R S and R cross S.

122
00:11:44,498 --> 00:11:48,978
The length of rho sigma is the length of rho plus the length of sigma.

123
00:11:49,428 --> 00:11:52,735
So by the Product Lemma, the series multiply.

124
00:11:53,185 --> 00:12:01,803
An unambiguous star satisfies the conditions of the String Lemma. So the series is one over one minus R of x.

125
00:12:02,253 --> 00:12:07,629
The basic expressions epsilon, 0 and 1 have series 1, x and x.

126
00:12:08,079 --> 00:12:12,602
By induction, the theorem holds for every unambiguous expression.

127
00:12:13,052 --> 00:12:21,564
Theorem 3.13 needs an unambiguous expression. Example 3.14 shows what goes wrong without it.

128
00:12:22,014 --> 00:12:31,273
Both expressions here produce the set of all strings of 1s. Its series is one over one minus x. Every coefficient is 1.

129
00:12:31,723 --> 00:12:41,792
Epsilon or 1, star, also produces this set. But each piece can be epsilon. So it produces each string in infinitely many ways.

130
00:12:42,242 --> 00:12:46,594
Its rational function is one over, one minus, one plus x.

131
00:12:47,044 --> 00:12:51,268
That is one over minus x, which is minus one over x.

132
00:12:51,718 --> 00:12:56,753
That says minus 1 objects of size minus 1. This makes no sense.

133
00:12:57,203 --> 00:13:02,408
1 or 11, star, gives one over one minus x minus x squared.

134
00:13:02,858 --> 00:13:06,762
The coefficients 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8 are wrong too.

135
00:13:07,212 --> 00:13:09,516
The coefficient of x cubed is 3.

136
00:13:09,966 --> 00:13:17,902
That is the number of ways the expression makes 111: 1 1 1, 1 then 11, and 11 then 1.

137
00:13:18,352 --> 00:13:25,072
So check a few small coefficients against a direct count. This catches an ambiguous expression.

138
00:13:25,522 --> 00:13:28,829
This slide is optional. It is not in the notes.

139
00:13:29,279 --> 00:13:35,657
The coefficient of x to the n in R of x counts the productions of length n, with repeats.

140
00:13:36,107 --> 00:13:43,126
Take 1111 in 1 or 11, star. One production is four pieces of 1.

141
00:13:43,576 --> 00:13:47,651
Three productions use one piece 11 and two pieces 1.

142
00:13:48,101 --> 00:13:50,938
One production uses two pieces 11.

143
00:13:51,388 --> 00:13:53,244
That is 5 productions.

144
00:13:53,694 --> 00:14:00,649
The coefficient of x to the 4 in one over one minus x minus x squared is also 5.

145
00:14:01,099 --> 00:14:10,720
When the expression is unambiguous, each string is produced once. Then the coefficient counts strings. That is Theorem 3.13 again.

146
00:14:11,170 --> 00:14:17,015
To prove that an expression is ambiguous, give one string and two different productions of it.

147
00:14:17,465 --> 00:14:26,425
For example, the string 00 in 0 or 00, star: 0 then 0, or the single piece 00.

148
00:14:26,875 --> 00:14:28,667
This is a complete proof.

149
00:14:29,117 --> 00:14:37,992
To prove that an expression is unambiguous, explain why each string has only one production. Then use Theorem 3.13.

150
00:14:38,442 --> 00:14:44,458
One common argument: read the string from left to right, and show that each piece is forced.

151
00:14:44,908 --> 00:14:48,471
Another: show that no piece is a prefix of another piece.

152
00:14:48,921 --> 00:14:52,825
Another: count something, for example the number of zeros.

153
00:14:53,275 --> 00:14:58,203
Another: show that the expression restricts a known unambiguous expression.

154
00:14:58,653 --> 00:15:05,074
Exercise 3.3 of the notes. A is 00 or 101 or 11.

155
00:15:05,524 --> 00:15:10,345
B is 00 or 001 or 10 or 110.

156
00:15:10,795 --> 00:15:20,310
Show that A star is unambiguous and B star is ambiguous. Then find the generating series of A star. Pause the video and try it.

157
00:15:20,760 --> 00:15:26,776
For A star, read from the left. If the next bit is 0, the piece must be 00.

158
00:15:27,226 --> 00:15:30,127
If the next bit is 1, look at the second bit.

159
00:15:30,577 --> 00:15:34,311
A second bit 0 means the piece is 101.

160
00:15:34,761 --> 00:15:37,641
A second bit 1 means the piece is 11.

161
00:15:38,091 --> 00:15:42,187
No piece is a prefix of another piece, so each piece is forced.

162
00:15:42,637 --> 00:15:50,210
Decode 1100101. It starts with 1 1, so the first piece is 11.

163
00:15:50,660 --> 00:15:53,924
Next comes 0, so the piece is 00.

164
00:15:54,374 --> 00:15:59,665
Next comes 1 0, so the piece is 101. There is only one way.

165
00:16:00,115 --> 00:16:09,053
So A star is unambiguous. A leads to x squared plus x cubed plus x squared, which is 2 x squared plus x cubed.

166
00:16:09,503 --> 00:16:17,354
By Theorem 3.13, the series of A star is one over one minus 2 x squared minus x cubed.

167
00:16:17,804 --> 00:16:26,380
For B star, look at the pieces 00 and 001. 00 is a prefix of 001.

168
00:16:26,830 --> 00:16:31,779
So the string 00110 is 001 then 10.

169
00:16:32,229 --> 00:16:35,536
It is also 00 then 110.

170
00:16:35,986 --> 00:16:38,973
Two productions, so B star is ambiguous.

171
00:16:39,423 --> 00:16:51,775
Practice problem P4-1, part a. Is 00 or 010, star, ambiguous? If it is not, find its generating series. Pause the video and try it.

172
00:16:52,225 --> 00:16:57,643
Read the string from the left. Each piece starts with 0. So look at the second bit.

173
00:16:58,093 --> 00:17:01,741
If the second bit is 0, the piece is 00.

174
00:17:02,191 --> 00:17:05,434
If the second bit is 1, the piece is 010.

175
00:17:05,884 --> 00:17:07,697
So each piece is forced.

176
00:17:08,147 --> 00:17:18,089
Decode 0 1 0 0 0 0 0 0 1 0. It starts with 0 1, so the first piece is 0 1 0.

177
00:17:18,539 --> 00:17:22,272
Next comes 0 0, so the piece is 0 0.

178
00:17:22,722 --> 00:17:26,242
Again 0 0, so the piece is 0 0.

179
00:17:26,692 --> 00:17:31,812
Then 0 1, so the last piece is 0 1 0. There is no other way.

180
00:17:32,262 --> 00:17:34,715
So the expression is unambiguous.

181
00:17:35,165 --> 00:17:41,096
The piece 00 leads to x squared, and the piece 010 leads to x cubed.

182
00:17:41,546 --> 00:17:48,223
By Theorem 3.13, the series is one over one minus x squared minus x cubed.

183
00:17:48,673 --> 00:17:53,132
Its coefficients are 1, 0, 1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3.

184
00:17:53,582 --> 00:18:03,011
Check one against a direct count. Length 5 has 2 strings: 00 then 010, and 010 then 00.

185
00:18:03,461 --> 00:18:08,389
Part b: 0 or 00, star. Pause the video and try it.

186
00:18:08,839 --> 00:18:12,594
The string 00 is produced as 0 then 0.

187
00:18:13,044 --> 00:18:16,777
It is also produced as the single piece 00.

188
00:18:17,227 --> 00:18:21,793
So the expression is ambiguous. This one string is the full proof.

189
00:18:22,243 --> 00:18:28,813
Part c: 00 or 01 or 10 or 11, star. Pause the video and try it.

190
00:18:29,263 --> 00:18:36,431
Every piece has length 2. So the cuts must be after every second bit, and each string is produced once.

191
00:18:36,881 --> 00:18:44,647
So it is unambiguous. There are four pieces, and each piece leads to x squared. The sum is 4 x squared.

192
00:18:45,097 --> 00:18:48,745
So the series is one over one minus 4 x squared.

193
00:18:49,195 --> 00:18:53,760
There are 4 to the k strings of length 2k, and none of odd length.

194
00:18:54,210 --> 00:19:03,298
Practice Midterm question 6, part a. Is this expression ambiguous or unambiguous? Prove it. Pause the video and try it.

195
00:19:03,748 --> 00:19:09,636
Look for a short string that two factors can both make: the middle star and the last factor.

196
00:19:10,086 --> 00:19:19,537
The middle star can make 111: one piece, with zero copies of 11 and zero copies of 0000.

197
00:19:19,987 --> 00:19:24,168
Then the first factor and the last factor both give epsilon.

198
00:19:24,618 --> 00:19:28,245
But the last factor can also make 111.

199
00:19:28,695 --> 00:19:38,999
111 star is 11 followed by 1 star. So it gives 11, 111, 1111, and so on.

200
00:19:39,449 --> 00:19:43,694
Then the first factor and the middle star both use zero pieces.

201
00:19:44,144 --> 00:19:49,051
So 111 has two productions. The expression is ambiguous.

202
00:19:49,501 --> 00:19:55,282
Write the answer like this: name the string, and write both productions factor by factor.

203
00:19:55,732 --> 00:20:04,201
Part b. We are told the expression is unambiguous. Find the generating series by length. Pause the video and try it.

204
00:20:04,651 --> 00:20:14,294
Translate from the bottom up. 011 leads to x cubed, and 111 star leads to one over one minus x cubed.

205
00:20:14,744 --> 00:20:18,285
Their product is x cubed over one minus x cubed.

206
00:20:18,735 --> 00:20:24,602
000 then 0 star leads to x cubed over one minus x.

207
00:20:25,052 --> 00:20:28,764
1 then 1 star leads to x over one minus x.

208
00:20:29,214 --> 00:20:33,545
Their product is x to the 4 over the square of one minus x.

209
00:20:33,995 --> 00:20:37,600
The or adds the two parts. Call the sum P of x.

210
00:20:38,050 --> 00:20:41,335
The star leads to one over one minus P of x.

211
00:20:41,785 --> 00:20:48,569
The last factor, epsilon or 0 or 00, leads to one plus x plus x squared.

212
00:20:49,019 --> 00:20:54,758
Multiply the two factors. The top of the answer is one plus x plus x squared.

213
00:20:55,208 --> 00:21:03,891
The bottom is one, minus x cubed over one minus x cubed, minus x to the 4 over the square of one minus x.

214
00:21:04,341 --> 00:21:14,410
By Theorem 3.13, this is the generating series. Its first coefficients are 1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 4, 8, 13.

215
00:21:14,860 --> 00:21:19,105
A direct count of the strings of each length gives the same numbers.

216
00:21:19,555 --> 00:21:29,923
To recap. A regular expression is built from epsilon, 0 and 1 with or, concatenation and star. It produces a rational language.

217
00:21:30,373 --> 00:21:35,899
The concatenation product loses the cut point, so it is not the Cartesian product.

218
00:21:36,349 --> 00:21:45,543
An expression is unambiguous if it produces every string exactly once. Lemma 3.9 checks this one operation at a time.

219
00:21:45,993 --> 00:21:53,524
To prove ambiguity, give one string with two productions. To prove unambiguity, show that each piece is forced.

220
00:21:53,974 --> 00:22:03,958
Translate with: epsilon to 1, each bit to x, or to plus, concatenation to times, and star to one over one minus R of x.

221
00:22:04,408 --> 00:22:13,261
For an unambiguous expression, this gives the generating series by length. For an ambiguous one, the result is meaningless.

222
00:22:13,711 --> 00:22:17,295
The next video covers block and prefix decompositions.
